Puzzle | Muddy Heads


A mother tells her two children, a boy and a girl, to play without getting dirty. However, while playing, both children get mud on their foreheads.
The mother says “At least one of you has a muddy forehead”. She then asks the children to answer “Yes” or “No” to the question: “Do you know whether you have a muddy forehead?”

The mother asks this question twice to each kid.
What will the children answer each time this question is asked, assuming that a child can see whether his/her sibling has a muddy forehead, but cannot see his or her own forehead? Assume that both children are honest and that the children answer each question simultaneously.

Solution:

Let s = "son has a muddy forehead"
Let d = "daughter has a muddy forehead"
The mother says, “At least one of you has a muddy forehead.” → This means s or d is true
1. First Round of Questioning:

Both children answer “No” to the question: “Do you know if you have mud on your forehead?”

Each child sees the other as having mud:

Son sees mud on daughter’s forehead → he knows d is true, but is unsure about s
Daughter sees mud on son’s forehead → she knows s is true, but is unsure about d
2. Second Round of Reasoning:

After hearing the son say “No”, the daughter reasons:

The son saw mud on her face (so he knows d is true)
He also knows s or d is true (from the mother’s statement)
If d were false, then s must be true → and the son would know he has mud → but he said “No”
So, d must be true → the daughter concludes she has mud
Similarly, after hearing the daughter say “No”, the son goes through the same reasoning:

If s were false, daughter would have known d must be true → but she said “No”
So, s must be true → the son concludes he has mud
3. Final Outcome:

On the second time the question is asked, both children answer “Yes” – they now know they have muddy foreheads.

